+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+
+faccessat2(F_OK) path lookup microbenchmark
+===========================================
+
+Motivation
+----------
+
+The ``bm_min_mysql_faccessat2_missing_1_0`` benchmark repeatedly issues
+``faccessat2(dirfd, "./var/lib/mysql/sbtest/", F_OK, AT_EACCESS)``.  The
+2026-06-09 run on a 32 CPU ``i9-13900K`` system shows a CPU-bound workload:
+``iostat`` reports no meaningful I/O wait and less than 2% NVMe utilization,
+while ``mpstat`` reports system CPU rising to roughly 76-78% at 30 workers.
+
+The full-system perf profile is dominated by VFS pathname lookup and access
+checking, not by ext4 writeback or block I/O:
+
+.. csv-table::
+   :header: "Path", "Approximate 30-worker sampled cycles"
+
+   "``do_faccessat()``", "81-82%"
+   "``filename_lookup()``", "49-50%"
+   "``path_lookupat()``", "46%"
+   "``link_path_walk()``", "28%"
+   "``getname_flags()``", "19-20%"
+   "``walk_component()``", "11-12%"
+   "``inode_permission()``", "10%"
+   "``strncpy_from_user()``", "9-10%"
+   "``lookup_fast()``/``__d_lookup()``", "5%"
+
+The native and container runs are close to each other, so the main scalability
+limit is shared VFS/namei CPU work rather than container isolation overhead.
+
+Implemented optimization
+------------------------
+
+``faccessat2(F_OK)`` verifies existence after pathname lookup.  The lookup
+itself has already checked search permission on parent directories.  After a
+successful lookup, ``do_faccessat()`` calls ``inode_permission()`` with the
+``MAY_ACCESS`` bit so that filesystem and security hooks still see an access
+probe.
+
+For inodes that do not implement a custom ``->permission()`` method, the VFS
+sets ``IOP_FASTPERM`` and falls through to ``generic_permission()``.  With a
+mask of exactly ``MAY_ACCESS``, ``generic_permission()`` does not enforce any
+DAC read, write, or execute bit because no such access bit is requested.  The
+new fast path therefore returns success from ``do_inode_permission()`` when:
+
+* the inode is already known to use the generic permission path, and
+* the requested mask is exactly ``MAY_ACCESS``.
+
+This removes repeated uid/gid mapping, ACL checks, and mode-bit calculations
+from hot existence probes while keeping the rest of ``inode_permission()``
+unchanged.
+
+Correctness and safety
+----------------------
+
+The change is deliberately narrow.
+
+* Filesystems with a custom ``->permission()`` method are unaffected.  The
+  fast path is reached only after ``IOP_FASTPERM`` is set, which means the inode
+  has no custom permission method.
+* Directory search permission is still checked during pathname walking through
+  ``may_lookup()`` and related namei code.  The patch affects only the final
+  ``MAY_ACCESS`` check after lookup succeeds.
+* ``sb_permission()`` still runs before the fast path.  Write checks, immutable
+  checks, and unmapped-id checks are unchanged for masks that request write
+  access.
+* Device-cgroup and LSM checks still run in ``inode_permission()`` after
+  ``do_inode_permission()`` succeeds.  This preserves security hooks such as
+  ``security_inode_permission(inode, MAY_ACCESS)`` and keeps IMA/LSM access
+  semantics intact.
+* Read, write, and execute checks are unchanged because the fast path requires
+  ``mask == MAY_ACCESS`` exactly.
+
+More aggressive ideas not implemented here
+------------------------------------------
+
+The profile also shows substantial time in ``getname_flags()`` and
+``strncpy_from_user()`` because the benchmark copies the same short userspace
+pathname on every syscall.  A kernel cache for userspace pathname strings would
+be unsafe without very careful invalidation because userspace can modify the
+buffer before each syscall.
+
+Similarly, caching the resolved path for repeated ``faccessat2()`` calls would
+need to handle rename, mount, idmapped mount, namespace, LSM, and permission
+changes.  That is far riskier than removing generic DAC work that is already a
+semantic no-op for ``MAY_ACCESS``.
+
+Validation guidance
+-------------------
+
+The targeted validation for this change is:
+
+* compile ``fs/namei.o`` with an x86_64 ``defconfig``;
+* run sparse over ``fs/namei.c``;
+* run VFS-focused selftests that cover path lookup and openat2 behavior;
+* run access/faccessat/faccessat2 smoke tests for existing, missing,
+  inaccessible, symlink, and empty-path cases;
+* rerun ``bm_min_mysql_faccessat2_missing_1_0`` and confirm that perf shows a
+  smaller ``generic_permission()``/``acl_permission_check()`` footprint while
+  LSM hooks remain visible when enabled.
